## Final Submission Summary\
\
- **Strongest Research Findings:** The industry is moving decisively toward multi-dimensional taxonomies. Separating the *Item Master* from *Menu Presentation* and *Location Overrides* is the only viable way to support both a single bakery and a 50-location hotel chain on the same platform.\
- **Areas with High-Confidence Evidence:** Global QSR category structures, major allergen regulatory standards (FDA 9), and the foundational role of specific East African dishes (Nyama Choma, Ugali, Rolex) are well-documented and uncontested.\
- **Areas with Weak or Incomplete Evidence:** Standardized digital POS taxonomy for traditional Burundian and Rwandan cuisine is sparse. Most data is derived from culinary travel guides rather than live commercial menu APIs, requiring PEWA to rely on "Candidate" or "Regional" status for these classifications until more local business data is onboarded.\
- **Most Important Recommendations:** \
  1. Adopt the "Core + Extension" taxonomy model to balance platform-wide searchability with local business autonomy.\
  2. Do not force East African staples into Western "Side Dish" categories; provide a dedicated "Foundation Staples" classification.\
  3. Implement strict separation between factual safety attributes (Allergens) and business-declared claims (Halal, Vegan).\
- **Questions Requiring Further Investigation:** \
  - How will PEWA's UI render multi-category items (e.g., a dish that is both a "Starter" and "Vegan") without cluttering the interface?\
  - What is the legal liability threshold for PEWA if a business incorrectly tags an item as "Nut-Free"? (Requires legal counsel review).\
\
*All research adheres to the boundaries set: no code, schemas, or binding commercial decisions have been prescribed. This report serves as a foundational, evidence-based reference for PEWA's taxonomy design.*}